WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
After 2012 nutrition standards, students wasted 31% of vegetables served in U.S. school lunches.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.340
This is unintended consequences, episode 175.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.800
The National School Lunch Program had long served more than 31 million children each

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.680
day. Earlier data still showed vegetables and fruit among the most discarded items

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.120
on those trays. Congress passed the Healthy Hunger-Free Kids Act in 2010 to align meals with

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.360
dietary guidelines for Americans and to address documented shortfalls in children's vegetable

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.400
and whole grain consumption. Staff at the U.S. Department of Agriculture,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:42.440
USDA, translated that statute into new minimum portion rules. The rules took effect in the 2012

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:48.740
to 2013 school year. Every reimbursable lunch had to contain at least a half cup of fruit or vegetable.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.740
Many menus offered both. Officials reason that if the items sat on the tray and counted toward

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.100
reimbursement. Students would select them and eat more of them.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.680
Plate-way studies from the 1990s and the 2000s had already shown children throwing away

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.600
large shares of produce when portions were smaller. Raising the required amount looked

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.120
on that record like a logical next step. The policy assumed availability and visibility

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.920
would become consumption. It treated the tray as a fixed container whose contents would

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.340
be accepted once they were present. It did not separately account for the time needed

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.740
to adjust preferences, or for a typical 20-minute lunch.

00:01:27.784 --> 00:01:33.884
In fall 2012, schools in all 50 states changed procurement, menus, and service lines to meet

00:01:33.884 --> 00:01:38.144
the new daily and weekly vegetable subgroups, and the larger fruit requirement.

00:01:38.704 --> 00:01:43.664
Directors interviewed by USDA reported that many students initially rejected the unfamiliar

00:01:43.664 --> 00:01:48.424
whole grain items, and the larger raw vegetable portions now placed on every tray.

00:01:49.004 --> 00:01:52.704
Some districts ran taste tests and menu redesigns in the first year.

00:01:52.704 --> 00:01:56.404
Others added the mandated items and recorded what came back.

00:01:56.984 --> 00:02:01.364
Early monitoring asked whether schools were serving the required quantities, not whether

00:02:01.364 --> 00:02:02.384
students were eating them.

00:02:03.064 --> 00:02:08.584
By spring 2013, directors in multiple regions noted the extra produce in waste bins at higher

00:02:08.584 --> 00:02:10.704
rates than the previous year's smaller portions.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:17.544
The 2014-2015 school nutrition and meal cost study later captured those patterns at national

00:02:17.544 --> 00:02:17.964
scale.

00:02:18.784 --> 00:02:26.704
Reembursement paid for the full serving on the tray, so kitchens had little latitude to shrink portions even when early returns showed the food untouched.

00:02:27.284 --> 00:02:33.204
A nationally representative study of more than 170 schools measured what the standards had been written to change.

00:02:33.744 --> 00:02:36.164
The goal was higher actual intake of produce.

00:02:36.904 --> 00:02:46.304
Measured consumption did not rise in proportion to the extra food that reached the tray, so the added volume increased the absolute amount of produce that left cafeterias as waste.

00:02:46.304 --> 00:02:53.590
Mathematica conducted the school nutrition and meal cost study for USDA and published it in 2019.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:59.210
Under the new rules, students discarded 29% of milk and 26% of fruit.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:07.630
A 2017 systematic review of 53 studies spanning 1978 to 2015 confirmed that fruits and vegetables

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:12.110
remain the highest waste categories both before and after the 2012 changes.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:20.350
In 2024, a PLOS-1 analysis of world wildlife fund data from 134 schools measured an

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:24.590
average of 2900s of a pound of total plate waste per student per meal.

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.470
One tenth of a pound of that waste was produce.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:33.090
Every reimbursable meal had to carry a full serving of each required item, so kitchens

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:37.070
placed more food on trays even when prior data indicated low acceptance.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:42.710
students, who had taken smaller helpings or skipped the vegetable, now faced larger portions

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:47.470
they were on a custom to finishing inside a short lunch. Younger students and students

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:53.430
in the earliest or shortest lunch slots generated the most waste. The extra discards concentrated

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:58.090
among the groups least likely to finish the new items. Schools that let students choose

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:04.210
their own milk quantity reported 76% less milk waste. The produce rules offered no parallel

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:09.890
flexibility. The added volume raised disposal costs and added to an environmental burden already

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:16.730
documented in the 2002 USDA Economic Research Service report to Congress. The gap between what was

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:21.510
served and what was eaten persisted even as some districts added share tables or classroom

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:27.490
tasting programs. The sequence started with a statutory mandate for minimum portions. It moved

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:32.650
through procurement contracts that delivered larger quantities. It ended at the tray where limited

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:39.716
time and established preferences decided what was actually consumed. After 2014, USDA issued

00:04:39.716 --> 00:04:45.676
implementation guidance that permitted limited-menu flexibilities. The core portion requirements stayed in

00:04:45.676 --> 00:04:51.736
place. Later studies tested longer lunch periods, student choice and portion size, and non-curricular

00:04:51.736 --> 00:04:59.056
prompts. Success at scale was mixed. The 2024-plus-1 paper noted that schools adopting at least

00:04:59.056 --> 00:05:03.476
one non-curricular intervention recorded significantly lower produce waste.

00:05:04.196 --> 00:05:08.516
Adoption stayed uneven across the more than 100,000 schools in the program.

00:05:09.116 --> 00:05:12.996
No comprehensive reversal of the 2012 produce mandates has occurred.

00:05:13.676 --> 00:05:15.856
The standard still shape what goes on the tray.

00:05:16.576 --> 00:05:18.536
Waste reduction remains largely local.

00:05:19.116 --> 00:05:23.676
The original rule still sets the floor for what kitchens must serve, while individual

00:05:23.676 --> 00:05:26.856
schools experiment with ways to cut what students discard.

00:05:26.856 --> 00:05:34.777
The people who set those minimums were answering a documented shortfall, with a tool the reimbursement system could actually check.

00:05:35.197 --> 00:05:40.237
When a rule requires a minimum quantity on every tray regardless of prior acceptance data,

00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:44.077
kitchens increase what is served even if consumption does not follow.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:49.637
The 2012 standards rewarded compliance with visible portions rather than measured intake,

00:05:50.137 --> 00:05:53.517
so the extra produce moved from delivery truck to tray to bin.

00:05:54.257 --> 00:05:58.997
Districts that later introduced choice in milk volume or share tables reduced waste only

00:05:58.997 --> 00:06:02.997
after they altered the rule that had fixed the amount placed before each child.

00:06:03.557 --> 00:06:06.777
A kitchen paid for what is visible will keep plating that amount.

00:06:07.357 --> 00:06:11.917
A child with about 20 minutes, especially a younger child in the shortest slot, will

00:06:11.917 --> 00:06:13.557
not automatically eat the difference.

00:06:14.217 --> 00:06:18.277
If a nutrition policy is written today, how will its success be defined?

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:22.197
By the weight that leaves the kitchen or by the weight that stays on the plate?

00:06:22.197 --> 00:06:26.317
We'll leave it there for today, thanks for thinking through this one with me.

00:06:26.717 --> 00:06:31.237
If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you

00:06:31.237 --> 00:06:32.157
tomorrow with another.

